#N canvas 0 0 720 700 10;
#X text 12 8 xm~ - XM/MOD/S3M tracker module player (libxm core);
#X text 12 28 One object does everything: load \, play \, mix \, split. Outlets 0/1 = the stereo mix (L/R - an unnormalized sum of up to 32 channel frames \, loud modules can exceed +-1). Outlets 2-33 = channels 1-32 in isolation (post volume \, post panning). Outlet 34 = bang at the module end without looping. This demo module has 4 channels - try wiring outlets 2-5 to their own gain chains.;
#X text 20 190 transport;
#X msg 20 210 load demo.xm;
#X msg 140 210 play;
#X msg 190 210 pause;
#X msg 240 210 stop;
#X msg 20 240 loop 1;
#X msg 100 240 loop 0;
#X msg 180 240 info;
#X msg 240 240 status;
#X msg 310 240 goto 1 0;
#X msg 410 240 mute 1 1;
#X msg 500 240 mute 1 0;
#X text 20 290 load resolves the file through the pd search path and the patch's own directory (.xm native\; .mod/.s3m load too \, non-XM accuracy is upstream best-effort). play restarts from the top \, pause keeps the position. loop 0 stops at the module's end - libxm's play-once semantics: the first time any pattern row is crossed again (the natural order wrap \, or a backward Bxx jump). goto seeks by position only - upstream: "seeking modules is broken by design" \, envelopes carry over.;
#X text 20 390 sync/other uses: `status` dumps every active channel (instrument \, frequency \, volume \, panning) on the nes~/opl3~ status convention\; the split outlets let you process each channel separately - filters \, delay throws \, a per-channel [output~]. Manual playing reuses the module's own instruments live: `chN instr x` picks one \, `chN note <midi> [vel]` plays it (C-4 = 60 \, the instrument's own keymap picks the sample) \, `chN off` releases \, `chN glide <ms>` arms a portamento time \, `chN slide <midi> [ms] [vel]` sweeps the sounding note to the pitch over ms milliseconds without a key event (vel 0-127 rides the volume column \, omitted = the level stays) \, `chN fx <hex>` runs any XM effect (C20 volume slide \, 404 vibrato \, 045 arpeggio). The first manual command freezes the sequencer so pattern rows cannot overwrite the notes - `play` resumes it. Bare spellings act on ch1\; `ch1 note 60 note 64 note 67` chains like opl3~;
#X text 20 460 wire it:;
#X obj 20 520 xm~;
#X obj 20 560 *~ 0.4;
#X obj 120 560 *~ 0.4;
#X obj 20 590 dac~;
#X text 260 560 outlets 2-33 = channels 1-32 alone \, outlet 34 = end bang;
#X text 20 622 manual playing - the sequencer freezes \, `play` resumes it:;
#X msg 20 647 ch1 instr 1;
#X msg 105 647 ch1 note 60;
#X msg 190 647 ch1 note 60 100;
#X msg 300 647 ch1 slide 67 300;
#X msg 395 647 ch1 off;
#X msg 460 647 ch1 fx C20;
#X msg 550 647 ch1 note 48 90;
#X connect 3 0 17 0;
#X connect 4 0 17 0;
#X connect 5 0 17 0;
#X connect 6 0 17 0;
#X connect 7 0 17 0;
#X connect 8 0 17 0;
#X connect 9 0 17 0;
#X connect 10 0 17 0;
#X connect 11 0 17 0;
#X connect 12 0 17 0;
#X connect 13 0 17 0;
#X connect 17 0 18 0;
#X connect 17 1 19 0;
#X connect 18 0 20 0;
#X connect 19 0 20 1;
#X connect 23 0 17 0;
#X connect 24 0 17 0;
#X connect 25 0 17 0;
#X connect 26 0 17 0;
#X connect 27 0 17 0;
#X connect 28 0 17 0;
#X connect 29 0 17 0;
